230 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 126 0 R
/E (Since I did not follow a tutorial for the hardware, the trickiest part was figuring out what materials to use and how to assemble the headphones. The Arduino Uno was definitely too big, so I'm glad I decided to go for the Nano, as it made hiding it much easier. The soldering was a little tricky as well, but everything worked out fine \(except for my first Nano, which I accidentally destroyed by getting solder on a wrong part...\).)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
231 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 126 0 R
/E (As long as the music is relatively loud, it can be about half a meter away to still be picked up, which I think is totally sufficient. I wore them for about an hour to take videos and pictures for Social Media, and they worked great.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 126 0 R
/E (If I were to make these again, I would like to learn how to 3d model to give the headphones a cleaner look. The microphone slightly picks up the noise from the servo, which I don't mind too much, as playing music overtones the servo. In some videos I took, I could see the blue cable that connects to the power bank. I will probably get a new, longer cable and paint it the color of the wig. Overall, I am really happy with how the headphones came out, and I think they are a great prop for cosplay.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
